window.SEARCH_INDEX=[{"path":"chapters/ch01-what-agentic-coding-is.html","title":"Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron Chapter 01 · What agentic coding actually is After this chapter you can explain what a coding agent really is — model, harness, loop — run your first session without getting burned, and name the moments when reaching for an agent is simply the wrong move. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 The hype flattened the language. \"The AI built my app\" can mean four different things: a chatbot answered while a human typed every change, an autocomplete guessed lines, a scripted pipeline transformed text, or an agent genuinely acted — read the repo, ran the tests, edited files, retried. Those are not shades of the same thing. This chapter builds the mental model everything else in this book sits on. Three parts, one machine Strip the marketing and every coding agent has three pieces. The model is the language model itself — a system that predicts likely text, one token at a time. It has no hands. It cannot open a file, run a command, or remember yesterday. Left alone, it produces text and stops. The harness is the program wrapped around the model. You never talk to a raw model; you talk to a harness that injects a system prompt you didn't write, hands the model tools — read this file, edit that function, run this command — enforces permission gates, manages the transcript so the model appears to remember the session, and feeds tool output back into the conversation. Practitioners describe a coding agent as exactly this: a harness wrapping a language model with invisible prompts and callable tools, where the ability to execute code is the defining capability. Two people using the identical model through different harnesses will have wildly different experiences — the harness is most of what you experience as \"the product.\" The loop is what makes it agentic. The working definition this book adopts: an agent is a language model running tools in a loop, toward a goal, bounded by a stopping condition. The model proposes an action, the harness executes it, the result feeds back in, and the wheel turns again — until the goal is met, the agent stalls, or the budget runs out. Remove the loop and you have a chatbot. A chatbot answers; an agent acts. scenario: clear goal — “make these three tests pass” vague goal — “make the login page better” run one pass reset scenario: clear goal · context 0% · passes 0 0% 25% 50% 75% 100% = context limit pick a scenario, then pace it one pass at a time · the meter scale is honest — 100% is the hard limit · run BOTH endings and compare them: that gap is the lesson Notice what is missing from that loop: magic. The agent is not thinking in any deep sense. Each pass, it looks at everything in its transcript and predicts a next action. The craft of this entire book is shaping what the loop can see, what it may touch, and how it knows it is finished. Here is the loop you own — wrapped around the machine's loop. Every technique in this book lives somewhere in this second chain. aim one goal, one success test → watch skim the plan before edits land → verify run the proof yourself → trust it? → commit accept, correct, restart clean one pass of the outer loop · human-gated · every step stays yours tip Write the stopping condition before you write the goal. \"Make the login page work\" is a wish; \"make these three tests pass without touching any other file\" is a stopping condition. Clear success criteria are the highest-leverage habit in agentic work — trial-and-error loops need a finish line they can actually detect, and vague goals are how loops wander. What makes it agentic — and when to skip it entirely An agentic coding session differs from chatting with a bot in three concrete ways: the agent reads your actual files, runs real commands, and iterates on its own output without waiting for you between steps. Official documentation for leading harnesses is blunt that this is a different working relationship, not a faster chat window — you manage it more than converse. There is also a distinction practitioners lean on constantly. A workflow is ordinary code orchestrating a model through steps a human already understands: summarize this, classify that, format the result. An agent is the model directing its own process, choosing which tools to run and when. The canonical guidance from Anthropic's effective-agents essay: start with the simplest thing that could possibly work, and reach for an agent only when the task genuinely needs flexible, open-ended execution — agents trade predictability for flexibility, cost more in latency and tokens, and compound errors along the way. Add complexity only when measurements say you must. Say no to the agent when: the steps are already known — a script or workflow is cheaper, faster, and cannot improvise surprises; latency or cost budgets are tight — a loop of model calls is expensive by construction; errors compound dangerously — anything irreversible on live systems belongs behind a sandbox and guardrails first; you cannot state what \"done\" looks like — an agent with no success criterion cannot stop honestly, and neither can you verify it. warning Autonomy amplifies whatever it touches, including mistakes. Field reports describe agents that skipped permission prompts, stalled on slow tools, or wandered when a refactor exceeded their capacity — and the security story is worse: a rogue agent with shell access is a classic injection target. Treat any \"skip approvals\" flag as radioactive outside a disposable, sandboxed environment. Guardrails are part of the design, not an optional extra. Three classic failure modes These recur so reliably across teams and harnesses that learning to spot them by name is worth the price of this chapter. Failure one: the kitchen-sink session. Five features go into one endless conversation. Context fills up, quality sags, the agent contradicts decisions it made an hour ago, and nobody can untangle the diff afterward. Official best-practices docs warn against exactly this shape. Fix: one goa"},{"path":"chapters/ch02-the-harness-landscape.html","title":"Chapter 2 · The harness landscape — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral Chapter 2: The harness landscape Promise: after this chapter you can pick a coding harness deliberately — instead of copying whatever is trending — install opencode or Gemini CLI for exactly $0, and explain what skills, plugins, MCP and LSP do before you touch any of them. facts checked as of 2026-08-25 · this landscape moves fast; the dates on every receipt matter First principles: what a harness decides for you A coding agent is two things wearing one trench coat: the model — the LLM doing the reasoning — and the harness : the program wrapped around it that turns a chat into somebody who can actually touch your project. Beginners assume the model is the product. Practitioners know the harness quietly decides most of the experience. Before naming a single tool, learn the three decisions every harness makes on your behalf. The tool loop The harness defines which tools the model may call — read files, edit files, run shell commands, search the web — how results feed back into the conversation, how many iterations are allowed, and when the agent must stop and report. Loop design is why two harnesses driving the identical model can feel like different products: one pauses to show you a diff after every edit, another barrels through twenty steps before surfacing anything. Your goal stated once, in words → Harness plans builds the prompt, picks tools → Model calls tools reads, edits, runs commands → Permission gate allow · ask · deny → You verify diff shown, loop repeats or stops the harness loop — owned by the tool around the model, not the model itself The permission model Every serious harness implements some version of allow / ask / deny : actions that always run, actions that prompt you first, and actions that never run. Where those lines sit is a values decision disguised as a settings screen. A harness whose default is \"skip the permission prompts\" is making a philosophical statement, not a technical necessity — and you inherit it unless you push back. Context handling The harness decides what enters the model's attention automatically: memory files, project instructions, skill descriptions, compiler warnings. It also decides what gets compressed away when a session grows long, and whether past sessions can be resumed or branched. This silently shapes output quality long before prompt-writing skill does — Chapter 5 goes deep on it. Four words you must be able to define You will meet these in every harness's feature list. Learn them tool-agnostically now: Skill — a written instruction file the harness loads on demand , teaching it a procedure (\"how we deploy\", \"how we name things\") only when relevant. Plugin — code you add that hooks the harness's own lifecycle events, enforcing rules or adding tools the authors never imagined. MCP — the Model Context Protocol, an open standard that lets a harness connect to external tool servers — databases, browsers, APIs — through one common socket. LSP — the Language Server Protocol: the machinery editors use for errors and \"go to definition\". A harness can tap those diagnostics so the agent sees the compiler's complaints directly. Watch the gate decide: a permission simulator No install needed for this one. Set the three gates below the way you believe a harness should behave, press run agent , and watch a three-step agent trace hit them one call at a time. You can even flip a gate mid-run — the next call obeys whatever the gates say at that moment . This is exactly what a harness's permission layer does for you on every single session, whether you ever look at it or not. permission gate simulator · interactive · runs locally, nothing leaves this page read_file(\"budget.csv\") pull a local file into the model's context ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny run_shell(\"python analyze.py\") execute a command on your machine ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny fetch_page(\"https://rates.example.org/current.json\") reach out to the network ✓ allow ? ask ✕ deny ▶ run agent reset gates trace idle — set the gates above, then press run agent. The landscape, honestly compared Nine harnesses were alive and documented at writing time, plus one instructive casualty. The table compares only what the sources below actually say — license, the genuine $0 path, and the one thing each does that others don't. Nothing here is sponsored opinion; every cell has a receipt. harness license $0 entry standout opencode (Anomaly) MIT Free OSS binary; local models or free-key clouds; reuses ChatGPT / Copilot / GitLab Duo subs; optional paid Zen/Go 75+ providers; TUI/desktop/IDE; skills + plugins + LSP; plan/build agents Claude Code (Anthropic) Proprietary No free tier; subscription or API credits One engine across terminal/IDE/desktop/web; subagents, Agent SDK, routines Codex CLI (OpenAI) Apache-2.0 Free install; ChatGPT-plan sign-in or usage-based API Same agent in CLI/IDE/app/Web cloud Gemini CLI (Google) Apache-2.0 Free tier: 60 req/min, 1,000 req/day with personal Google OAuth Largest gratis allowance + 1M-token context Cline Apache-2.0 (JetBrains plugin closed) Free OSS; BYO keys / OpenRouter / local VS Code + JetBrains + headless CLI + Kanban board + SDK; Plan/Act toggle Goose (Block → AAIF/Linux Foundation) Apache-2.0 Free OSS; BYO keys, 15+ providers; sub reuse via ACP Native desktop + CLI + embeddable Rust API; recipes, 70+ MCP extensions Aider Apache-2.0 Free OSS; BYO incl. Copilot auth, OpenRouter, Ollama Git-native pair programming: auto commits, repo map, edit-format leaderboards Cursor CLI Proprietary · licensing unverified — check ToS before relying Requires Cursor account; free-tier limits not confirmed on the CLI page Editor-parity Agent/Plan/Ask in the shell; & hands a task to the Cloud Agent Pi (Mario Zechner) MIT ( @mariozechner/pi-coding-agent ) Free OSS; BYO keys via the pi-ai multi-provider layer Minimalist 4-tool core (read/write/edit/bash); TS extensions; deliberately no baked-in subagents or plan mode Roo Code — archived historical row Apache-2"},{"path":"chapters/template.html","title":"Chapter template — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"part I · tools & access · L1 coral Chapter N: Title goes here One-line promise: what the reader can DO after this chapter. facts checked as of YYYY-MM-DD Generalized first, always The technique is taught tool-agnostically before any specific harness is named. This section would explain the concept, why it matters, and the mental model — readable by someone who has never installed anything. tip Practitioner shortcuts live here. Earned, not invented. warning Common failure modes and how to recognize them early. grey zone · risk-flagged Anything ToS-adjacent or unofficial lives in risk callouts: what it is, why people do it, what breaks, what it could cost you. Never recommended without the warning attached. Demonstration on the build The same technique applied inside the current threaded project, with real commands, real output, and honest notes about what went wrong the first time. $ command that a reader can paste verbatim expected output shown honestly, including errors Receipts Every factual claim above traces to a source card like these: T1 official https://opencode.ai/docs/skills checked 2026-08-24 T2 vendor blog https://example.com/marketing-post checked 2026-08-24 · marketing claims flagged inline T3 lead only https://example.com/forum-rumor never cited as fact — pointer for your own verification Try it yourself Exact-click checkpoint. Open X, click Y — written so a non-coder follows mechanically. Break it on purpose. Change one thing, predict the result, check your prediction. Ship-sized task. Apply today's technique to your own project, not just the book's. Self-check Comprehension question one? Answer, in plain language. Comprehension question two? Answer. When would this technique be the wrong choice? Answer. Beyond this build Where else this pattern applies, which other harnesses support it (and how their mechanics differ), and the edge cases where it breaks down. This block is the generalization guarantee — never omit it. ← contents next chapter →"},{"path":"index.html","title":"The Agentic Coding Field Manual — अ Man Learns Things","text":"अ Man Learns Things · a field manual · scaffold v1 The Agentic Coding Field Manual Master the modern agentic workflow — prompting patterns that survive production, UI craft, testing discipline, daily habits — with coding agents like opencode, Claude Code, Codex and Gemini CLI. $0-first tools, receipts behind every claim. Written for people who learn by building, not by reading theory. Open the contents Watch it being built 16 chapters 3 real builds $0-first tools blind-critic quality gates The method that wrote this book Every chapter is ground through the same loop you'll learn inside it: a builder agent makes a piece, a separate critic with fresh context compares ours against the real reference blind, picks a winner, names the biggest gap, and sends it back. The loop only stops when ours wins. builder writes the piece → critic fresh context, harsh, binary blind A/B vs bar → biggest gap named, sent back loop until ours wins — never a round count Three real builds carry the book You won't practice on toys. Three projects from Aman's own build specs get constructed chapter by chapter as the book teaches — each one $0-native, each one shipped. build 1 · weekend Agent Cost Compass Paste your agent's token pattern, see what a run costs across ~20 LLM providers. Your first agent build — configured, prompted, verified, shipped. build 2 · two-week NCERT Quiz Forge Quizzes + spaced repetition for NCERT textbooks. Where UI craft, testing discipline and human-review gates get learned for real. build 3 · six-week capstone Free-Tier Atlas The IMDb of free tiers — change-tracking, watchlists, community submissions. Pipelines, anti-bloat ops and honest grey zones, at capstone scale. Generalized, not project-bound Every chapter teaches the technique tool-agnostically first, demonstrates it on the build, then closes with Beyond this build : where else the pattern applies, which other tools it works with, and where it breaks. The coverage matrix in docs/ guarantees nothing gets dropped because a project didn't happen to need it."},{"path":"progress.html","title":"Progress — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Progress The book is built in phases, each phase a fresh gauntlet run that resumes from disk. This board is the human view of STATUS.md — agents update both together at every milestone. last updated: 2026-08-25 · P1 research wave complete Done P0 · shipped Scaffold Repo + local git, design tokens (light + dark), site skeleton, client-side search, theme toggle, coverage matrix, governance files. P1 · shipped Research wave Six research agents vetted sources per chapter cluster. ~160 tiered rows in the source ledger; every future citation comes from it. Ten gaps logged with binding drafting instructions. P2 · wave 1 · shipped Chapters 01–02 won their gauntlets Ch 01 took three rounds — the critic forced an interactive loop lab where a clear goal exits honestly at 36% context and a vague one dies at the 96% wall. Ch 02 won in two with a permission-gate simulator you can flip mid-run. Build 1 M1 scaffolded at Projects\\agent-cost-compass. In transit P2 · now Wave 2 + your verification gate Chapters 03–04 draft next. Meanwhile: open agent-cost-compass and run the five hand-checks from the M1 review packet — your approve unlocks M2 (real pricing data + calculator). Not yet built P2 cont. Foundations finish Chapters 03 (models without money) and 04 (configuring your agent) gauntleted; agent-cost-compass woven through as its milestones pass gates. P3 Craft + Build 2 Chapters 5–11; ncert-quiz-forge built. P4 Ship & sanity + Build 3 Chapters 12–16; free-tier-atlas capstone. P5 Integration & launch Cross-links, full-book blind judging vs both bars, push to GitHub, Pages live. How quality is enforced builder agent fresh context → critic agent fetches real bars blind A/B, labels stripped → verdict winner + biggest gap bars: ciechanow.ski (visual) · code.claude.com best practices (content)"},{"path":"toc.html","title":"Contents — The Agentic Coding Field Manual","text":"Contents Six parts on six route lines. Statuses update as phases land; rows become links when their chapter ships. part 0 · orientation · L0 saffron 01 What agentic coding actually is v1 · won its gauntlet Harness vs model vs loop. Mental models that survive contact with real work, the failure modes nobody blogs about, and when NOT to reach for an agent. Includes an interactive loop lab: run the same bug with a clear goal vs a vague one. part I · tools & access · $0 core · L1 coral 02 The harness landscape v1 · won its gauntlet opencode deep-dive; Claude Code, Codex CLI, Gemini CLI, Cline, Goose, Aider, Cursor CLI, Pi compared on what matters at $0. Includes a permission-gate simulator you can flip mid-run. 03 Models without money planned Free tiers, OpenRouter free models, open-weight GLM/Qwen/Kimi/ DeepSeek, local via Ollama & LM Studio, subscription reuse, rate-limit reality. 04 Configuring your agent properly planned AGENTS.md & memory files, skills, custom commands, MCP servers, hooks as guardrails, permissions, LSP diagnostics, plan mode, sessions and worktrees. part II · prompting craft · L2 olive 05 Context engineering planned What enters context, compaction, memory across sessions, token discipline, context rot and how to fight it. 06 Patterns that ship planned Plan-first, spec-driven development, gauntlet loops and evaluator patterns, TDD with agents, subagent fan-out, verification loops, anti-patterns. 07 Source literacy for agent builders planned Reliable vs unreliable sources, how benchmarks get gamed, verifying claims yourself in minutes. part III · building real things · L3 jade 08 Good UI with agents planned Design tokens and systems, screenshot-compare loops, component contracts, accessibility as a default not an afterthought. 09 Testing what agents build planned Test strategy for agent output, browser automation, review gates a non-coder can run, evals-lite. 10 Debugging when it breaks planned Systematic debugging with agents, bisection, reading logs, escape hatches when the loop spins. 11 Brownfield reality planned Working inside large existing codebases: understand-before-edit, safe refactors, mapping unfamiliar territory. part IV · shipping & staying sane · L4 plum 12 Pipelines that prevent bloat planned Branching discipline, CI gates, agent code review, dependency hygiene, refactor loops, docs drift. 13 Shipping for $0 planned GitHub Pages, Cloudflare Pages, Vercel free paths, preview deploys, release notes written by agents. 14 Security & secrets planned Prompt injection, secret leakage into context, malicious MCP servers and skill supply chains, permission deny lists. 15 Costs, limits & grey zones planned One compact reference chapter: rate-limit math, spend caps, free-tier tradeoffs and ToS boundaries — the honest footnote, not the theme. 16 Your daily agentic workflow planned The capstone habit: session rituals, memory upkeep, parallel worktrees, review rhythm — how competent people actually work with agents every day. appendices · reference shelf · L5 rose A Tool cheat sheet planned One-page-per-harness command and config reference. B The source ledger planned Every source cited in the book, tiered and dated. C Glossary planned Harness, context window, MCP, hook, skill — plain definitions. D Coverage matrix planned Dimensions × chapters — proof of exhaustiveness, living document."}];
